            <ArticleTitle>Dyslipidemia in patients with angiographically confirmed coronary artery disease--an opportunity for improvement.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>577-80</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">There are few data about lipid profiles in unselected patients with angiographically confirmed coronary artery disease (CAD).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="HYPOTHESIS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The study was undertaken to investigate the demographics, clinical characteristics, angiographic findings, and baseline lipid status of 1,000 consecutive unselected patients with angiographically confirmed CAD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Between April 2001 and July 2002, we obtained informed consent and prospectively collected clinical characteristics, fasting lipid profiles, and angiographic results from 1,000 sequential patients with CAD confirmed by angiography.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In these patients with confirmed CAD, 78% had history of hyperlipidemia. Although 62% were receiving lipid-lowering therapy, only 46% had a low-density lipoprotein target of &lt; 100 mg/dl, and only 20% had achieved all four National Cholesterol Education Program-recommended lipid targets.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Better strategies to ensure optimal lipid levels are required. One such method using computerized workflow is being evaluated in this population.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
